  <p class="note">
    <strong>Status: pre-1.0.</strong> The interfaces move. Version numbers are cut when a
    consumer needs one, not when an API is declared stable. The crates are used from the
    Git repository; none is published to a package registry yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Why it exists</h2>

  <p>
    3D Tiles is the dominant format for streaming large geospatial 3D scenes, and its
    ecosystem is lopsided. Producing tilesets is a solved problem: several mature,
    independent tilers write static trees of <code>tileset.json</code> and glTF payloads.
    Consuming them is concentrated in a small number of clients, each welded to the
    ecosystem it was written for.
  </p>

  <p>
    Tuile is the engine without the ecosystem attached: one core that decides what a camera
    needs, at what detail, within what memory, and hands decoded geometry to whatever draws
    it. Interactive viewing, a film rendered frame by frame and an offline path tracer are
    <em>modes</em> of that core, not separate products — a feature built for one of them
    goes into the shared layers so every other gets it, and two modes that disagree on
    screen is a bug.
  </p>

  <p>
    The other hole is serving. There is no lightweight dynamic server for 3D Tiles — one
    binary, several sources, a cache you can swap or remove. That server is designed and
    its seams are kept open, but it is <strong>not built yet</strong>; the
    <a href="/roadmap">roadmap</a> says where it stands.
  </p>

  <h2>What is in the box</h2>

  <div class="grid">
    <div>
      <span class="name">tuile-core</span>
      <p>
        3D Tiles 1.0 and 1.1 parsing with external tilesets, glb and b3dm decoding,
        screen-space-error traversal, a deterministic graphics budget, and the geometry
        server. No renderer, no async runtime, no HTTP client. Compiles to
        <code>wasm32-unknown-unknown</code>, and CI enforces it.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">GeometryStream</span>
      <p>
        The streaming protocol as a core trait, not a network API. The geometry server runs
        in-process, or in a browser worker behind <code>postMessage</code>; the renderer
        cannot tell them apart.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">The globe</span>
      <p>
        Quantized-mesh terrain draped with raster imagery, walked by the same traversal as
        3D Tiles. Sources from connectors, from any <code>layer.json</code> and XYZ template,
        or from a heightfield of your own served over plain HTTP — no token required.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">tuile-wgpu</span>
      <p>
        The reference renderer on wgpu 30: Metal, Vulkan, OpenGL ES, WebGPU and WebGL2. It
        executes the budget's plan, never leaves the ground black, and can draw into a frame
        that a host engine owns — its device, colour and depth.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">Viewers</span>
      <p>
        One application, four shells: desktop (with a macOS application bundle), iOS,
        Android, and the browser as an ES module — WebGPU, or WebGL2 where there is none,
        its geometry server in a Web Worker.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">Compressed imagery</span>
      <p>
        Imagery held on the GPU as BC7 or ASTC blocks — a quarter of the memory — encoded on
        the GPU, in the page's workers, or transcoded from stored KTX2. The client's choice,
        off by default; anything that cannot be encoded stays RGBA8.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">Films</span>
      <p>
        Record a flight, bake its tiles once, render it frame-exact on a laptop or on a farm
        of GPUs — with radiometric harmonisation of the imagery and a physical sky. One
        render mode among the others.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="name">OpenUSD</span>
      <p>
        A recorded camera path written as a USD stage in pure Rust, and a Hydra plugin over
        a C ABI that streams terrain and imagery into whichever Hydra renderer draws it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p>
    In review: <code>tuile-bevy</code>, the globe inside a Rust game engine, drawn by
    <code>tuile-wgpu</code> on the engine's own device and targets. The full list, with what is
    shipped and what is not, is on the <a href="/architecture">architecture</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2>The rules it is built under</h2>

  <p>
    These are not style preferences. Each one is load-bearing, and each one has cost
    something to learn.
  </p>

  <ol class="rule-list">
    <li>
      <strong>The core depends on no backend.</strong> No renderer, no async runtime, no
      HTTP framework, no filesystem on production paths. Input and output cross named
      traits — <code>TileFetcher</code>, <code>ContentStore</code>,
      <code>GeometryStream</code>. Wanting to add a heavy dependency to the core is the
      signal that an abstraction is in the wrong place.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The core compiles to WebAssembly.</strong> Checked on every change. It is the
      cheapest possible proof that rule one still holds.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>All geospatial arithmetic is 64-bit until rebasing.</strong> Earth-centred
      coordinates run to about 6.4 million metres; 32-bit floats resolve roughly half a
      metre there, which is visible jitter. Positions are rebased onto a local origin in
      f64 and only then handed to the GPU as f32.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ground is never black.</strong> A soft, blurry or stale tile is always
      preferable to an absent one: a replacement is created and activated before what it
      replaces is switched off, and there is no frame in between.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>What crosses a network is a standard format.</strong> Tilesets, glb,
      quantized-mesh, image tiles — never a private wire format, so any conforming producer
      can feed Tuile. The decoded form exists only in memory, on the consumer's side.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The project defines itself by the standard.</strong> Not against any product.
      The normative reference is
      <a href="https://docs.ogc.org/cs/22-025r4/22-025r4.html" rel="noopener">OGC 3D Tiles 1.1</a>.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      A Rust developer who wants a terrain globe, or a 3D Tiles scene, drawn inside their
      own wgpu application or game engine without embedding a C++ engine.
    </li>
    <li>
      Someone who needs the same globe on a desktop, a phone and a web page, from one
      codebase, with the memory budget each device can afford.
    </li>
    <li>
      Anyone who wants a flight over real ground rendered as a film, or in an OpenUSD
      renderer, frame-exact and reproducible.
    </li>
  </ul>

